      "body": "Key Takeaways:\n\nAdvanced packaging pushes process behavior across company lines, so what one supplier hides now shapes another company’s prediction.\n\nEncryption and abstraction protect the recipe cleanly, but neither tells the receiving engineer where the model stops being valid.\n\nA model can be accurate and still be wrong for the question asked, making its envelope as much a deliverable as the model.\n\nThe goal seems simple — share the model without sharing the process. A materials supplier shouldn’t have to reveal a proprietary formulation just so a customer can predict how it will behave in a package, and a foundry shouldn’t have to hand over its recipe to provide a useful model of a chiplet. But advanced packaging is making that separation much harder to maintain.\n\nFor years, engineers moved much of what they needed between companies through datasheets, design rules, and qualification specs, while the manufacturing knowledge stayed with the supplier. That arrangement held as long as",
